#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110599 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110334 :question/id #uuid "4cc37ad7-b382-5b7e-bcda-92b2818c5650" :question/math-academy-id "e-14359" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y= -\\dfrac{2}{5}x-3?$\n\n- $y = 5x + 1$\n\n- $y = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3$\n\n- $y = -\\dfrac{x}{2} + 5$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nThe line $y=-\\dfrac{2}{5}x-3$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-\\dfrac{2}{5}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3$ has a slope of $\\dfrac{5}{2}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\left( -\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{5}{2} = -1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y = \\dfrac{5}{2}x + 3$ is perpendicular to the given line.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29005c66-9f30-5862-926f-8a09320d404c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da24a9f5-3957-5314-9170-30b884dd9a23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 3x + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110060} {:answer/id #uuid "e1709a04-a50a-5140-8508-4d40aeaad7c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-x + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110061} {:answer/id #uuid "1f88b987-a713-58fb-8345-e889baf81949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-3x + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110062} {:answer/id #uuid "212a853a-a60b-5b0e-b2b7-e66cfb2dc466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{3}x + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110063} {:answer/id #uuid "589a45ca-ab3d-5fb9-b633-eedc7cfc9c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{3}x + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110064}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2503a0b6-3750-5f80-90a0-5811e5d68622"