pe/math} :answer/value "y=-4x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295724} {:answer/id #uuid "54639a79-a283-5c4f-98c5-d6bf46bb4a73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{4}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295753} {:answer/id #uuid "985f1a86-4c26-587d-bc4e-21443b145563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296081} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cdd7ce-2038-589e-9329-77831ef5c644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54639a79-a283-5c4f-98c5-d6bf46bb4a73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{4}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295753} :answer-field/id #uuid "b480cea5-ce9a-5d35-a098-8db89b92e2bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297012}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab7f99bc-8f2b-5a80-8f9e-dc70e299882e" :question/math-academy-id "q-28341" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $16x+4y=12?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $16x+4y=12,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16x+4y & =12 \\\\ 4y & =-16x+12 \\\\ y & =-4x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-4.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{x}{4}-5$ has a slope of $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{x}{4}-5$ is perpendicular to the given line."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f20bfc5-25a1-57c7-bd09-e8e6b0a21865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330366} {:answer/id #uuid "84d0ec45-5ed5-5df5-8bf1-4fd0e1c45ff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331130} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3b2964-17e7-5808-a701-9f9137ce9817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331609} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6a0e31-3e49-5057-ace0-6f12af2104b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2}x-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331781} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ff3419-17c7-5b79-9e28-cd231b2a431e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc3b2964-17e7-5808-a701-9f9137ce9817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331609} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9ad3706-ddcd-54c4-a1b1-d8e0952fecfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332639}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15d0899e-a286-517c-afed-bf0fea233f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-59763" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $4x+2y=8?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $4x+2y=8,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2y & =8 \\\\ 2y & =-4x+8 \\\\ y & =-2x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-2.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+5$ has a slope of $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+5$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110601 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110335 :question/id #uuid "a53a3337-108b-5129-b203-ff70ce8d0abe" :question/math-academy-id "e-14362" :question/problem "The lines $y=2x+9$ and $3y+kx = 12$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=2x+9$ is $2.$\n\n- To find the slope of  $3y+kx = 12,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+kx & =12 \\\\ 3y & =-kx+12 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{k}{3}x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $3y +kx = 12$ is $-\\dfrac{k}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot\\left(- \\dfrac{k}{3}\\right) = -1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k = \\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f3d7d62-686b-59c4-9ea1-695c6be3180b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110946 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "44ff4e88-f963-54b8-a7b4-3ad7020873e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107532}] :question/id #uuid "e007d387-871b-581a-84e0-e5a3ebc663b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-215415" :question/problem "The lines $y = \\frac{1}{3}x + 2$ and $y = kx - 1$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "11067ef1-9a47-5ef7-a541-73aad937b3c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107533}] :question/id #uuid "1f5622d8-91e2-5b94-8209-977a38e1136e" :question/math-academy-id "q-215416" :question/problem "The lines $y = 3x - 5$ and $tx + 6y = 4$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $t$?$t =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "a114c460-8ce0-5155-b75a-453eef90ed24" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107534}] :question/id #uuid "f28c59aa-1035-5b80-91ed-14aa2887f369" :question/math-academy-id "q-215417" :question/problem "The lines $2x - 3y =-6$ and $kx + 2y = 2$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234324 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fc754ae-de19-52ab-9f2e-a42c4822e26f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72548" :question/problem "The lines $2y+10x=3$ and $y=px-3$ are perpendicular. The value of $p=$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The second line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=px-3$ is $p.$\n\n- To find the slope of  $2y+10x=3,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+10x & =3 \\\\ 2y & =-10x+3 \\\\ y & =-5x+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $2y+10x=3$ is $-5.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n-5\\cdot p=-1\\,⟹\\,p=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbe9c7df-3baf-502a-bdf1-650e5114f4ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbe9c7df-3baf-502a-bdf1-650e5114f4ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289694} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bee18c5-a61f-5ef1-8b3d-563d9d708f2a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289831}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac5a3f61-2785-57a6-8ba3-6a2018ca7d25" :question/math-academy-id "q-215939" :question/problem "The lines $y=2x+1$ and $kx-10y=3$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=2x+1$ is $2.$\n\n- To find the slope of $kx-10y=3,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-10y & =3 \\\\ 10y & =kx-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{k}{10}x-\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $kx-10y=3$ is $\\frac{k}{10}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n2\\cdot (\\frac{k}{10})=-1\\,⟹\\,k=-5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80e55783-b575-58b9-a332-72a7c4d966f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80e55783-b575-58b9-a332-72a7c4d966f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288901} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ff07bfd-cbbf-59c2-b98c-cd9fdfb57c50" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290048}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "136056a6-37b6-586a-94ae-13431344c9a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-215941" :question/problem "The lines $y=-5x+3$ and $3kx-5y=9$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=-5x+3$ is $-5.$\n\n- To find the slope of $3kx-5y=9,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3kx-5y & =9 \\\\ 5y & =3kx-9 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3k}{5}x-\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $3kx-5y=9$ is $\\frac{3k}{5}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-5)\\cdot (\\frac{3k}{5})=-1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "476917e8-859c-5503-88ab-ca2e9ff2c81e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288522}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "476917e8-859c-5503-88ab-ca2e9ff2c81e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288522} :answer-field/id #uuid "c91da3c3-adba-5714-9611-6c6417b39dc2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290168}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07f8e54a-3bdb-557d-bce5-8db99b8641ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-215942" :question/problem "The lines $y=7x-2$ and $kx-14y=7$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\n- The first line is in slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So the slope of the line $y=7x-2$ is $7.$\n\n- To find the slope of $kx-14y=7,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-14y & =7 \\\\ 14y & =kx-7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{k}{14}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line $kx-14y=7$ is $\\frac{k}{14}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n7\\cdot (\\frac{k}{14})=-1\\,⟹\\,k=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45f3d3f7-094d-5cdf-a1eb-5fae715041c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45f3d3f7-094d-5cdf-a1eb-5fae715041c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288508} :answer-field/id #uuid "813457e6-0980-5145-90b0-80eb70b3f992" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290013}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "647405ca-0083-5cb8-98f4-79f7db403388" :question/math-academy-id "q-215944" :question/problem "The lines $10x+5y=3$ and $2kx+y=2$ are perpendicular. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of $10x+5y=3,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x+5y & =3 \\\\ 5y & =-10x+3 \\\\ y & =-2x+\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of $2kx+y=2,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2kx+y & =2 \\\\ y & =-2kx+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The slope of the line $10x+5y=3$ is $-2.$\n\n- The slope of the line $2kx+y=2$ is $-2k.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-2)\\cdot (-2k)=-1\\,⟹\\,k=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55f88508-da2b-5f50-bc9a-a9d26b49e411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293282} {:answer/id #uuid "a5105cfc-01bb-5d42-a770-d43d047ecbd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293581} {:answer/id #uuid "c8156b99-af7d-5f7b-b0a7-f383c4b1a5c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293707} {:answer/id #uuid "c91b613b-66f6-5d95-9a11-12d165d11100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293713} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ecb80b-49f9-59d8-9960-66dfad7c060e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8156b99-af7d-5f7b-b0a7-f383c4b1a5c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293707} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2a93840-02de-536d-9650-a5ee63bece85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294727}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe99c4a9-356e-5f86-a2a8-a7dd24fba9f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-28328" :question/problem "Given that the lines $y=5qx+9$ and $y=-3x-8$ are perpendicular, what is the value of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nBoth lines are slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. \n\n- The slope of the line $y=5qx+9$ is $5q.$\n\n- The slope of the line $y=-3x-8$ is $-3.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n5q\\cdot (-3)=-1\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c5cea50-fecb-5d5b-8a89-f4f34d327f19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328005} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3ca05e-2e03-5005-8439-1e9b0e51d16a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328028} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d0efcf-7b5d-51ae-a082-657d77e63c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328768} {:answer/id #uuid "df927e48-fbac-512c-b73b-41214fbca6fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328892} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e47ca3-6ef9-5cec-9235-9aa609798010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f3ca05e-2e03-5005-8439-1e9b0e51d16a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328028} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e4084a6-ffaf-52c0-9e02-59ab5e2b3839" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329328}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa337919-23eb-5736-a04b-9af0d92af77d" :question/math-academy-id "q-44407" :question/problem "The lines $4x-8y=7$ and $3y-px=9$ are perpendicular. The value of $p=$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of $4x-8y=7,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-8y & =7 \\\\ -8y & =-4x+7 \\\\ 8y & =4x-7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of $3y-px=9,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-px & =9 \\\\ 3y & =px+9 \\\\ y & =\\frac{p}{3}x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The slope of the line $4x-8y=7$ is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of the line $3y-px=9$ is $\\frac{p}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{p}{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,p=-6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cf2b17a-0b7e-5754-a062-d7e5b5df2294" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327467} {:answer/id #uuid "488f1a1a-e425-56a3-81e8-9c76b21913b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327565} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb9c31a-4ef7-5c9f-8a15-22e0a9155430" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327739} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7ce0bf-2bd7-5bb4-a6af-40e080f96b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328171} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e7e926-8dc2-5aec-9b5d-5cedd3d46127" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e7ce0bf-2bd7-5bb4-a6af-40e080f96b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328171} :answer-field/id #uuid "08474740-7cae-5d8e-b313-be0b9c41d244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329198}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1f9e8da5-beaf-5a86-b98c-192a93fc99b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-44515" :question/problem "The lines $15x+5y=9$ and $4y+3px=0$ are perpendicular. The value of $p=$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of $15x+5y=9,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15x+5y & =9 \\\\ 5y & =-15x+9 \\\\ y & =-3x+\\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of $4y+3px=0,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y+3px & =0 \\\\ 4y & =-3px \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3p}{4}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The slope of the line $15x+5y=9$ is $-3.$\n\n- The slope of the line $4y+3px=0$ is $-\\frac{3p}{4}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-3)\\cdot -\\frac{3p}{4}=-1\\,⟹\\,p=-\\frac{4}{9}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Unknown Parameters Given Two Perpendicular Lines"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110602 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110336 :question/id #uuid "5eca5ef2-627f-5d32-8edb-88e223b11d71" :question/math-academy-id "e-14365" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $x=2$ and passes through the point $(3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since the line $x=2$ is a vertical line, a perpendicular line would need to be horizontal. So, the perpendicular line must take the form $y=k$ for some value of $k.$\n\nThe perpendicular line needs to go through the point $(3,4),$ which has a $y$-coordinate of $4.$ So, the perpendicular line is $y=4.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6fee8d5-e1f7-599d-a74c-abc44d973065" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "9d51b4bc-0806-5b57-a4a9-30e6fb4bca40" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107535} {:answer-field/id #uuid "a15e1a9b-1d3d-5c59-960a-9ee83d2d311b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107536}] :question/id #uuid "4195803a-42bd-5c36-83eb-3efb2b176331" :question/math-academy-id "q-215418" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y = 2$ and passes through the point $(-3, 4)$. Write your answer in **standard form.** {{field-1}} $=$\n{{field-2}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110950 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "bc25fe25-0330-5b6d-a1df-77f042d7d394" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107537} {:answer-field/id #uuid "e5e28cc4-7edb-5d4a-86c2-ccea3087d4d7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107538}] :question/id #uuid "822bd19c-5832-5995-9033-2d980ce20a91" :question/math-academy-id "q-215951" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $x = \\frac{1}{3}$ and passes through the point $(7, 6)$. Write your answer in **standard form.** {{field-1}} $=$\n{{field-2}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f0066a1-769b-592f-8e8c-b8e5ce9fdeb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244452} {:answer/id #uuid "815c0ca9-6b07-51b1-aca2-d92948bb5797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 1