4eaa9-084c-53b2-8078-d461a7041d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-44691" :question/problem "Determine the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-7,-9)$ and is perpendicular to $y=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the line $y=5$ is a horizontal line, a perpendicular line would need to be vertical. So, the perpendicular line must take the form $x=k$ for some value of $k.$\n\nThe perpendicular line needs to go through the point $(-7,-9),$ which has an $x$-coordinate of $-7.$ So, the perpendicular line is $x=-7.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110372 :question/id #uuid "6355ac1e-bb4d-5133-9917-04dfb2bc5a0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-18854" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$\n\n- $-3x = -4x + 6$\n\n- $2x + 7 = 2x - 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & =4x+1 \\\\ 4x+1-4x & =4x+1-4x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x & =-4x+6 \\\\ -3x+4x & =-4x+6+4x \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-3x = -4x + 6$ has the unique solution $x = 6.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+7 & =2x-1 \\\\ 2x+7-2x & =2x-1-2x \\\\ 7 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f2ddfed-e12b-5737-9067-c75298c26d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295538} {:answer/id #uuid "319f007b-2777-5802-a5f3-be9d57acd10e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295547} {:answer/id #uuid "7511c6ff-fcd8-5956-97ad-15896c224ffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295905} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7fb40a-bcf8-56b5-ba17-de4d716ae6e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296108} {:answer/id #uuid "c98b46de-a866-5e57-8204-c176ef5cf504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f2ddfed-e12b-5737-9067-c75298c26d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295538} :answer-field/id #uuid "6993e9c7-57b5-5671-8f39-f711bb76c56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296854}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71c3d793-6b55-5392-ac94-1149b022497f" :question/math-academy-id "q-287966" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $2x=x+1$\n- $2x+1=2x+1$\n- $2x+1=2x+3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =x+1 \\\\ 2x-x & =x+1-x \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x=x+1$ has the unique solution $x=1.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =2x+1 \\\\ 2x+1-2x & =2x+1-2x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $2x+1=2x+1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =2x+3 \\\\ 2x+1-2x & =2x+3-2x \\\\ 1 & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d518381-826d-5b5a-9143-c3384f54726d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295343} {:answer/id #uuid "25bcd93e-20ab-5b9d-bad7-604d1797c45a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295474} {:answer/id #uuid "9d301123-07d0-56ec-a5c4-7f7c478e79f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296106} {:answer/id #uuid "cebb4fa4-d8f2-535b-ba82-cf8af4934ae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296350} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0e031d-7889-5551-b460-079399aa3aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d301123-07d0-56ec-a5c4-7f7c478e79f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296106} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d2a9183-8ac0-595e-a28c-d41eacaa0077" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296753}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b24019f-f592-514c-8874-109e97014d8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-288419" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $3x=2x+5$\n- $4x-2=4x-2$\n- $5x+3=5x+1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =2x+5 \\\\ 3x-2x & =2x+5-2x \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3x=2x+5$ has the unique solution $x=5.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2 & =4x-2 \\\\ 4x-2-4x & =4x-2-4x \\\\ -2 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x-2=4x-2$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+3 & =5x+1 \\\\ 5x+3-5x & =5x+1-5x \\\\ 3 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b3b264-c3bf-56ca-98eb-2d6cd21c3128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295408} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee88acd-7941-541d-92ae-327eb954fb4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295723} {:answer/id #uuid "920b99e8-cc75-5101-9bba-6a0d521d38ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296052} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6ea962-b6ce-5bb8-b5bf-25d51a764721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296184} {:answer/id #uuid "f2861c84-edf3-5ebd-851c-60ce99e270e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17b3b264-c3bf-56ca-98eb-2d6cd21c3128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295408} :answer-field/id #uuid "51c2d36a-6f01-5415-a69e-1a9749544603" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296796}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5426119-4ea0-5c84-bb6c-cf460c622db0" :question/math-academy-id "q-288425" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $-3x+2=-3x+7$\n- $6x+1=6x+5$\n- $7x-4=6x-6$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+2 & =-3x+7 \\\\ -3x+2+3x & =-3x+7+3x \\\\ 2 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+1 & =6x+5 \\\\ 6x+1-6x & =6x+5-6x \\\\ 1 & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-4 & =6x-6 \\\\ 7x-4-6x & =6x-6-6x \\\\ x-4 & =-6 \\\\ x-4+4 & =-6+4 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $7x-4=6x-6$ has the unique solution $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f2cb23e-a320-550f-80fb-5d57177ced2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295443} {:answer/id #uuid "5a31613b-8925-5a47-a2df-6b3f46bb6e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295785} {:answer/id #uuid "7935fd04-86fe-5c9d-b74f-227c3df9fdc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295929} {:answer/id #uuid "e40fb2df-e9bd-55aa-a47c-3fc4ba486d2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296484} {:answer/id #uuid "ef187862-df62-575f-8ffd-89e5afb16587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a31613b-8925-5a47-a2df-6b3f46bb6e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295785} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df2639b-eb03-5fd2-8163-d1dd5acd6070" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296924}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31ef6d07-7ebc-5067-b0a5-3f361bec8d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-288427" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $2x-5=x-2$\n- $6x+1=6x+9$\n- $-4x+3=-4x+3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =x-2 \\\\ 2x-5-x & =x-2-x \\\\ x-5 & =-2 \\\\ x-5+5 & =-2+5 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-5=x-2$ has the unique solution $x=3.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+1 & =6x+9 \\\\ 6x+1-6x & =6x+9-6x \\\\ 1 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+3 & =-4x+3 \\\\ -4x+3+4x & =-4x+3+4x \\\\ 3 & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-4x+3=-4x+3$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5987c83e-257d-55b4-a17e-3965dafe501e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295783} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd73961-4fa5-55a6-b016-4ed50daa78c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295946} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5b127e-bdcf-5063-975e-7d2cfc4e8774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296166} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9ae661-533a-5359-845d-1a9edbbbdc06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296526} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6316b3-6aa1-5dae-ba84-4457b158e8a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb6316b3-6aa1-5dae-ba84-4457b158e8a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296606} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3dc02a4-0b73-558c-b7e7-05f609d48e61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296979}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43eccfac-bd80-5f7b-abff-3250eee5dc70" :question/math-academy-id "q-288591" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\n- $3x+2=4x+1$\n- $2x-5=3x-3$\n- $5x-4=5x-4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =4x+1 \\\\ 3x+2-3x & =4x+1-3x \\\\ 2 & =x+1 \\\\ 2-1 & =x+1-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3x+2=4x+1$ has the unique solution $x=1.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =3x-3 \\\\ 2x-5-2x & =3x-3-2x \\\\ -5 & =x-3 \\\\ -5+3 & =x-3+3 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-5=3x-3$ has the unique solution $x=-2.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-4 & =5x-4 \\\\ 5x-4-5x & =5x-4-5x \\\\ -4 & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $5x-4=5x-4$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110368 :question/id #uuid "4a8f8deb-2ff1-56d2-888a-3e51eb805bc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-17212" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(5s + 4)x = 9x\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na = 5s + 4, \\qquad b = 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5s + 4 = 9.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5s & =5 \\\\ s & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ca1cf7-eae8-588f-b62e-92e2eed7ae06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295403} {:answer/id #uuid "5f7c70a7-d1b8-56a0-9961-6101a0fea82a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295813} {:answer/id #uuid "89099718-058c-53aa-ac2e-3dc7b59c4b5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296004} {:answer/id #uuid "a1cb3a02-6977-565b-8c6f-64a04e91a8d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296129} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf45a98-a317-5ebe-a0ee-d58863e54e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16ca1cf7-eae8-588f-b62e-92e2eed7ae06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295403} :answer-field/id #uuid "78f9540e-726b-5906-87cd-68bbcee4ccc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296880}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "573bc8f1-012d-5d7e-834a-c6ec17bb0b63" :question/math-academy-id "q-287970" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2px=3x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2p,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2p=3.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a449b2-0875-581b-a2ec-8813a227c1de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295383} {:answer/id #uuid "2d6f283f-3592-5248-baf7-ca782b7d8c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295530} {:answer/id #uuid "4009ef03-e229-5324-9dbd-214305b60cd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295633} {:answer/id #uuid "4dfca85a-c698-5e36-ba67-02a55f783f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295715} {:answer/id #uuid "d199324f-9733-59f2-82cf-8e4f45ae01ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14a449b2-0875-581b-a2ec-8813a227c1de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295383} :answer-field/id #uuid "1739b830-13d4-586f-9761-4372557e83e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296679}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "435ae769-5577-5924-a2b3-9f23f7e0ccc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-287971" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(p-3)x=4x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=p-3,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\np-3=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=7.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b30a8cc-6928-5b29-8e9d-969094b2881b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b30a8cc-6928-5b29-8e9d-969094b2881b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295513} :answer-field/id #uuid "f87dd3f0-9ada-5f78-bd01-d45ae62ce046" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297144}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e508284d-0585-5c0f-a542-d91d66f78ddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-287975" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(2p-3)x=5x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2p-3,\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2p-3=5.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p & =8 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45491d55-84a5-5641-85ee-02ca1a1e90b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295660} {:answer/id #uuid "7b982f8e-1429-5175-9e47-06c33c917451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295944} {:answer/id #uuid "9130ffb1-ff3d-56f6-b44a-a46263f888e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296047} {:answer/id #uuid "9540e0f1-3b23-5210-976d-85a05ecc13df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296063} {:answer/id #uuid "f61f19b9-1bc9-5327-81ed-c1e60a23106f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9540e0f1-3b23-5210-976d-85a05ecc13df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296063} :answer-field/id #uuid "057a3936-10b0-5e7b-83aa-558ff78363fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296650}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "556d6e5b-c151-55e9-9606-63f27cad6bd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-288436" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n5px=10x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=5p,\\,b=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5p=10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26fe00c2-ff7d-5b62-96ab-0af209fd24e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295480} {:answer/id #uuid "389f9ac4-b9fe-5bf6-9689-5a77751235bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295586} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd45365-694b-59a1-9c75-444ba21c1ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295949} {:answer/id #uuid "bc73343c-08ba-5843-8a40-f11269cd8d67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296258} {:answer/id #uuid "ff514a4f-a0d5-5889-8487-a38b01139445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cd45365-694b-59a1-9c75-444ba21c1ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295949} :answer-field/id #uuid "632eea42-d449-505d-9c6f-a3dc34d529ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296837}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e80edf76-d1e3-576c-b5c7-753b9159f67b" :question/math-academy-id "q-288440" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n-4kx=8x\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-4k,\\,b=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n-4k=8.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\nk=-2.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110721 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110371 :question/id #uuid "6eeec12a-5da8-5113-916a-58f4ca807207" :question/math-academy-id "e-18852" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nk + 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n3r = 12.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k = 2$ and $r = 4,$ we have\n\n$$\nk + r = 2+4 = 6.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2918787-6369-5281-b839-56303be60818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108557} {:answer/id #uuid "d22c7204-b52f-5b6a-ad06-733bfdfad3e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108558} {:answer/id #uuid "e529c4c3-c68a-5582-8d90-3a6410fd1ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108559} {:answer/id #uuid "074e8b23-4661-5096-bb58-43d425517222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108560} {:answer/id #uuid "558a10b7-3a92-55d7-9829-746619fff524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108561}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c9db9fdf-89d7-5da6-8efa-fb1d0de77d4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108556}] :question/id #uuid "46a7b671-3423-5c64-a7e7-5db3d2823ec3" :question/math-academy-id "q-287978" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$(p - 3)x + q = 4x + 1$\nwhere $p$ and $q$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p + q$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "e1aa3a94-3d74-527f-9f2b-4344ff0ea098" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108562}] :question/id #uuid "680bddd9-26ae-52c5-946c-8e72aafff50a" :question/math-academy-id "q-287985" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$(p - 5)x + 2q = 2x + 6$\nwhere $p$ and $q$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p + q$?$p + q =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11383c9f-6bca-51a0-bf87-839bec637ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11383c9f-6bca-51a0-bf87-839bec637ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295365} :answer-field/id #uuid "b78bfb02-4c20-5a75-811c-305086ea1983" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297016}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73256cc5-b18c-5ffc-bb1b-9018e84f6eb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-288583" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2tx+(1-m)=6x+4\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ and $m$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t+m?$\n\n$t+m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $t$ and $m$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n2tx+(1-m)=6x+4\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n2t=6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=3.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n1-m=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\nm=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $t=3$ and $m=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\nt+m=3+(-3)=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe4c39bf-4560-5825-a22a-76a5df230cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe4c39bf-4560-5825-a22a-76a5df230cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296621} :answer-field/id #uuid "58355121-2d5d-5aa8-91c2-79f41befa005" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296811}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1e37e75-7d0c-5923-8a40-5f706dc21da1" :question/math-academy-id "q-288584" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+3)x+2t=x+6\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s+t?$\n\n$s+t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+3)x+2t=x+6\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n-s+3=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\ns=2.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n2t=6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $s=2$ and $t=3,$ we have\n\n$$\ns+t=2+3=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26dea449-5daa-571d-bc24-21b16885571b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295478} {:answer/id #uuid "46ff9640-80f1-5703-b7a0-6219bb604868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295672} {:answer/id #uuid "7f368c30-e2fd-5534-8546-adacd3bdc62c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295955} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0e5e07-5208-59c1-8031-79b63eb57a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296248} {:answer/id #uuid "e8efa7fd-e32d-5724-96bb-c481dd8ea2c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46ff9640-80f1-5703-b7a0-6219bb604868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295672} :answer-field/id #uuid "049c5b1b-3107-5777-ad7f-5a1a5ac925c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296646}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "afc322c5-285c-5560-a114-d1da26bf18af" :question/math-academy-id "q-288622" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(m+2)x+5n=7x+15\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m+n?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $m$ and $n$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(m+2)x+5n=7x+15\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+2=7.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n5n=15.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $m=5$ and $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+n=5+3=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a9032f-3b49-520d-8dd2-f9561ab549a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295402} {:answer/id #uuid "408f08fe-9474-5041-b518-50e39558bff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295635} {:answer/id #uuid "40aa1a16-e6d9-5660-9646-91aa012afb7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295636} {:answer/id #uuid "4376d57c-3e21-5f7a-9e08-da1e663ee90a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295647} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e2b538-9abc-5649-871f-12082724e81b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4e2b538-9abc-5649-871f-12082724e81b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296563} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7f50aa1-8f5f-55b0-9f35-0014c59522a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296988}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48147a3d-8103-52a6-b2f5-5907d423b5d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-288623" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2kx+r=9x+6\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n2kx+r=9x+6\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n2k=9.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\nr=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=\\frac{9}{2}$ and $r=6,$ we have\n\n$$\nk+r=\\frac{9}{2}+6=\\frac{21}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28da790f-20c9-5875-b417-1be764fa2aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295496} {:answer/id #uuid "8db21277-c307-578a-8ebf-d5c8733da1ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296020} {:answer/id #uuid "c24c121c-9aae-5ae4-989f-173c011f7cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296294} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c8cfa6-04bf-59ad-b50b-09698209ddbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296316} {:answer/id #uuid "e7994d65-33f9-5bec-9e19-0969a17a2b2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8db21277-c307-578a-8ebf-d5c8733da1ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296020} :answer-field/id #uuid "a71ebbc6-abec-50c8-b8f1-d671ed8c0cf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296986}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f55693d-3dd0-5310-a538-5dcb7f69af97" :question/math-academy-id "q-288627" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}x+q=4x+20\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ and $q$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p+q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $p$ and $q$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}x+q=4x+20\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\nq=20.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $p=8$ and $q=20,$ we have\n\n$$\np+q=8+20=28.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110754 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110456 :question/id #uuid "42313322-7535-5969-ad84-958449e48c1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3480" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\dfrac{5}{9}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1 .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\dfrac{5}{9}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t        x^2+\\left(\\dfrac{5}{9}\\right)^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2+\\left(\\dfrac{25}{81}\\right)&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2&= 1-\\dfrac{25}{81}\\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2&= \\dfrac{56}{81}\\[5pt]\n\t\tx&= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{56}{81}}\\[5pt]\n\t        x&= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.\n}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive. So, we select the positive answer. \n\nTherefore, our final answer is $x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110987 :questio